# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-autocad-hung-instance-blocks-com-bootstrap — a stuck acad.exe the sweep cannot end refuses every later COM session
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* ANSWERED 2026-09-27 — the theory is refuted, and the real cause is fixed (2.2.131)

The observation this ticket asked for happened: =verify:autocad-activex:windows=
on PF5S26B, job 16761201255, clautolisp 2.2.129. Of the three outcomes listed
under /What remains/ it is the SECOND — the bootstrap fails with the rejection
while the sweep reports *no live instance*. So the hypothesis in the title is
wrong, and the cause is elsewhere. Concretely:

#+begin_example
cad sweep: 0 ended, 5 already settled, 0 still running     <- nothing alive
...
ATTACHED=0
CREATED=1                                                  <- it is OUR instance
alfe[warn]: backend AUTOCAD: process exited after 28.99 s; last status = "BOOTING"
bridge-autocad.vbs(217, 1) (null): L'appel a ete rejete par l'appele.
--- protocol\status.txt
BOOTING
#+end_example

=ATTACHED=0= is the fact that settles it: the bridge attached to nothing and
*created* the instance itself, and then that instance rejected a call. There was
no other AutoCAD to hold the registration — the sweep in the same job says so
independently. "A stuck acad.exe refuses every later COM session" describes
something that did not happen.

*And the rejection is TRANSIENT.* The same pipeline's =verify:epure-api:windows=
(job 16761201252) reached =READY after 30.95 s= and completed a request round
trip against a real AutoCAD. Two invocations, minutes apart, same machine: one
rejected at 29 s, one ready at 31 s.

** The real cause: the calls AFTER TouchApp were unguarded

=TouchApp= already retries =Visible = True= ten times (defect 2 of this ticket,
fixed earlier). What it did not cover is everything that follows it:

#+begin_src vbscript
WaitQuiescent app, waitSecs
If app.Documents.Count = 0 Then      ' <- unguarded
  Call app.Documents.Add("")         ' <- unguarded
End If
Set doc = app.ActiveDocument         ' <- unguarded, emitted line 217
#+end_src

A freshly =CreateObject='d AutoCAD answers =Visible = True= and is *then* still
opening its startup document and running its startup routines, so the next call
gets =RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED=. With no =On Error= around it the script died on a
RAW VBScript runtime error naming only a line number — not a diagnosis, and the
reason this read as "something is wedged" for a week.

Fixed: those three calls now sit in a 20-attempt, one-second retry loop that
reports, when it truly gives up, that the instance is up but never became ready
to take a command — and names a startup dialog on the desktop as the usual
reason, which is the same conclusion =cad-runner-wedged-by-modal-dialog= reached
from the other side.

*The misdiagnosis was also in the code, and is fixed.* =TouchApp='s failure
message said "Another instance is probably wedged and holding the registration:
end it on the machine" *unconditionally* — advice that is simply false when
=created= is true, and precisely the sentence that sent this investigation after
a phantom. It now branches on =attached=: blame another instance only when we
attached to one, and otherwise say that it is our own instance, still starting
or waiting on a dialog.

Two tests on the emitted template (=backend-cad-tests.lisp=): the structural one
requires an error guard between =WaitQuiescent= and the =ActiveDocument= call —
which would have failed before this change — and the other requires the advice
to branch. A wording note earned the hard way: assertions on the emitted VBS
must use fragments that are contiguous in the template, because =& _=
continuations split a sentence into several strings; the first version of both
tests failed on its own wording rather than on the code.

alfe suite: *1328 checks, 0 failures, SBCL and CCL.*

** What remains

- [ ] A re-run of =verify:autocad-activex:windows= reaching READY, to confirm
      the retry closes the gap on the invocation shape that failed. The
      evidence says the server becomes ready at around 30 s and the loop now
      waits up to 20, so this is expected rather than hoped — but it is a
      machine answer and has not been given yet.
- The title is now wrong and deliberately left alone: renaming it would break
  every reference, and the body says plainly that the premise is refuted.

* Observed (as filed — the reading of this evidence was WRONG, see above)

verify:epure-api:windows on master (alfe 2.2.96, job 16753534728,
2026-09-26 11:30). Both AutoCAD runs died in BOOTSTRAP:

#+begin_example
alfe: alfe AUTOCAD backend error (phase BOOTSTRAP): AutoCAD COM bridge
  (cscript) exited before READY (exit 0):
  ...\bridge-autocad.vbs(189, 1) (null):
  L'appel a ete rejete par l'appele. [PROCESS-EXITED-BEFORE-READY]
#+end_example

"L'appel a ete rejete par l'appele" is =RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED= (0x80010001):
the COM server refused the call. The cause is in the same trace, in the
sweep that runs before every Windows CAD job:

#+begin_example
sweep: 97 instance(s) recorded
sweep: ending orphaned acad.exe pid 21928 (created 20260925132249...)
sweep: pid 21928 survived Stop-Process
sweep: ending orphaned acad.exe pid 17640 (created 20260925140116...)
sweep: pid 17640 survived Stop-Process
   ... (97 "survived Stop-Process" lines) ...
sweep: 0 ended, 97 left
#+end_example

Two acad.exe from the PREVIOUS DAY (13:22 and 14:01 on 2026-09-25) are
still running and survive Stop-Process every time. A hung AutoCAD owns
the =AutoCAD.Application= COM registration, and it is that instance
=GetObject=/=CreateObject= reaches -- so it answers RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED
(it is modal, or wedged) and every subsequent AutoCAD session fails
before READY, whatever the session asks for.

So this masks everything else on the AutoCAD side: the EPURE API
question cannot be answered on AutoCAD while those two processes exist.

* Two defects, and they compound

1. *The sweep cannot end a hung instance.* =scripts/sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1=
   uses =Stop-Process -Force=, reports "survived", and proceeds. It also
   reports each of the 97 recorded entries separately although they are
   two distinct PIDs -- the created-CAD registry accumulates an entry per
   session and is never pruned, so the log is 97 lines for 2 processes.
2. *The bridge treats RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED as fatal.* A rejected call is
   the documented "server is busy" answer in AutoCAD automation, and the
   standard handling is to retry with a short back-off (that is what an
   =IMessageFilter= does for a native client). Failing the bootstrap on
   the first rejection makes a transiently busy AutoCAD indistinguishable
   from a wedged one.

* Directions

- Escalate in the sweep: after Stop-Process fails, try
  =taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F=; if the process still exists, FAIL THE JOB
  EARLY with a message naming the PIDs, instead of letting a doomed
  AutoCAD bootstrap run and report a COM error that looks like alfe's
  fault. A hung CAD is a runner-hygiene problem and must be reported as
  one.
- Prune the created-CAD registry: drop entries whose PID no longer
  exists, so the log reflects reality and the sweep does not re-report
  the same two processes 97 times.
- Retry RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED in bridge-autocad.vbs (a few attempts, ~1 s
  apart) before giving up, and say in the final message how many
  attempts were made.
- Then re-run verify:epure-api:windows to get the AutoCAD half of the
  EPURE API answer (alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation).

* Note

The same job answered the BricsCAD half: the file run reached READY in
25.7 s and reported =DONE 1 FAIL= with exit 1, which is the 2.2.91 fix
working on a real CAD -- before it, that run was a silent success.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.102: all three directions implemented, NONE
  verified on the machine

Both defects named above are addressed, and the third direction (pruning
the registry) with them. What cannot be done from here is the
verification: that runner's AutoCAD is the thing that does not start, and
the machine was asleep for this work. Everything below is therefore
written and unit-tested, and still has to be seen working on PF5S26BT.

*The sweep escalates and then refuses* (=scripts/sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1=):

- =taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F= after =Stop-Process= fails -- the stronger
  hammer, and it takes the process's children with it;
- if the process is STILL there, the sweep FAILS THE JOB (exit 1) naming
  each surviving PID and its creation stamp, and saying that they hold the
  COM registration so an AutoCAD session would only report a rejected
  call. A doomed bootstrap no longer runs and no longer looks like an alfe
  defect. =CAD_SWEEP_IGNORE_SURVIVORS=1= is the escape hatch for a job
  that wants to proceed anyway;
- the recorded entries are DEDUPLICATED on PID + creation stamp, and the
  count of raw lines is printed beside the count of instances. That is the
  97-lines-for-2-processes noise: the registry gains a line per session
  and was replayed in full.

*The bridge retries a rejected call* (the emitted bridge-autocad.vbs):
=TouchApp= wraps the FIRST call on the app object (=app.Visible = True=,
which is where the rejection landed) in ten attempts a second apart,
because RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED means BUSY -- a modal dialog, a load in
progress -- and a native client would have an IMessageFilter retry for it.
The give-up message says how many attempts were made and that another
instance is probably wedged.

Test: =autocad-bridge-retries-a-rejected-com-call= pins the helper, the
guarded first touch, the absence of the bare =app.Visible = True=, the
attempt count in the message, and that the file is still ASCII-only
VBScript that cscript can parse. alfe suite 1189 checks, 0 failures.

** CORRECTION 2026-09-26 — "wedged" was the wrong word: those processes are DEAD

The escalation above ran on the runner today, 22 times, and its conclusion was
wrong every time. =taskkill= answered, for each of pids 21928, 17640 and a new
9164:

: Raison : Il n'existe aucune instance en cours de cette tâche.

/There is no running instance of the task./ The processes had ALREADY EXITED.
Windows keeps the process-table entry until the last handle to it closes, and
=acad.exe='s parent here is the COM service, which holds one — so a dead
=acad.exe= keeps answering =Get-Process= for days. The sweep asked only whether
the id answered, called that "survived", and failed the job. Fixed in
clautolisp 2.2.110 (=cad-sweep-fails-jobs-for-zombie-pids=): the state is now
classified explicitly and only a genuinely =live= instance fails anything.

*What this does to THIS ticket's evidence.* Two of its claims must be held more
loosely until seen again:

- "the two wedged processes hold the COM registration, so an AutoCAD session
  would only report a rejected call" — a DEAD process holds no registration and
  answers no call. Whatever produced =RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED= earlier, it was not
  those two corpses, or not only them. The message the sweep prints (kept, it
  is right for a real survivor) must not be read back as evidence.
- "the two processes may need ending by hand" — they cannot be ended at all;
  they are already gone. Nothing needs doing on the machine for them, and a
  reboot is what will clear the table entries.

That leaves the original symptom — AutoCAD not starting under COM — WITHOUT a
confirmed cause. The retry (=TouchApp=) still stands on its own: a rejected
call means BUSY and a native client would retry it. But the "another instance
is wedged" explanation is now a hypothesis again.

** What remains

- *Observe it once on a clean runner.* Play =verify:autocad-activex:windows=
  (or any AutoCAD job) on clautolisp 2.2.110 or later. Three outcomes, all
  informative: the sweep reports the three ids as exited and the bootstrap
  SUCCEEDS (the corpses were never the problem and the earlier rejection was
  transient busy-ness, which =TouchApp= now waits out); or the bootstrap fails
  with the rejection while the sweep reports NO live instance (the cause is
  elsewhere — this ticket then needs reopening on a different theory); or the
  sweep names a genuinely live instance (the original theory, finally with
  evidence).
- Do NOT play it while another master pipeline is about to start: an
  interruptible pipeline cancels queued jobs, which has already cost this
  investigation two runs.

*Sharper, after that cost a third run (2026-09-27): do not play it on a MASTER
child at all.* =.gitlab/native.yml= sets =default: interruptible: true= (the fix
for the job-activity-cap leak), so a CAD job played on master's native child is
cancelled the moment ANY other session merges to master — which on a busy evening
is every ten minutes, and it is not something to time. Play it on a
*merge-request* child instead: a master merge does not supersede a different ref,
so an MR child survives. That is the same property that makes those children leak,
which is why the sweep since 2.2.127 spares a child holding a *played* job
(=pending=/=running=, as opposed to an unplayed =manual= one) — a queued CAD job
is no longer swept away either.

A plain branch will NOT do: =detect:runners=, which =native:pipeline= needs, runs
only on master, tags and =merge_request_event=, so an arbitrary branch gets no
child at all.

*And read =contacted_at=, not =status=.* A runner reports =status: "online"= for up
to two hours after it stops polling. PF5S26B and thalassa are intermittent and go
quiet overnight: on the night of 2026-09-26/27 they last polled at 23:27Z and
00:01Z, and 193 windows-tagged jobs sat =pending= with nothing running at all. The
two evidence jobs (=verify:autocad-activex:windows=,
=verify:epure-api:windows=) are PLAYED and queued in the child of !300 — they will
run when that machine comes back, and the sweep will not take them.
